Looking at the code:

```c
int
flash_swap_append(const struct flash_swap_ops *ops, unsigned int offset,
    const unsigned char *source, unsigned int length, unsigned char *scratch)
{
	unsigned int chunk, index;

	if ((offset & (FLASH_PROG_BYTES - 1)) != 0)
		return -1;

	while (length != 0) {
		if ((offset & (FLASH_SECTOR_BYTES - 1)) == 0 &&
		    ops->erase(offset, FLASH_SECTOR_BYTES) != 0)
			return -1;

		chunk = length;
		if (chunk > FLASH_PROG_BYTES)
			chunk = FLASH_PROG_BYTES;
		if (chunk == FLASH_PROG_BYTES) {
			if (ops->program(offset, source, FLASH_PROG_BYTES) != 0)
				return -1;
		} else {
			for (index = 0; index < FLASH_PROG_BYTES; index++)
				scratch[index] = 0xff;
			for (index = 0; index < chunk; index++)
				scratch[index] = source[index];
			if (ops->program(offset, scratch, FLASH_PROG_BYTES) != 0)
				return -1;
		}
		offset += FLASH_PROG_BYTES;
		source += chunk;
		length -= chunk;
	}
	return 0;
}
```

Before erasing:
- It checks `if ((offset & (FLASH_SECTOR_BYTES - 1)) == 0)` — i.e., the offset is aligned to a sector boundary.

Before programming:
- It checks `if ((offset & (FLASH_PROG_BYTES - 1)) != 0)` at the start — i.e., the offset is aligned to a program page boundary.

What callers must guarantee:
- The offset is aligned to a program page boundary (checked by `flash_swap_append` itself).
- The caller must guarantee that the offset is aligned to a sector boundary before calling `ops->erase()`.

